const body = `<h1>9.7 — Computer Systems: What Runs Your Game</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    60 frames per second means the machine has 16.67 milliseconds to
    run YOUR Update, the physics, and a million pixels. Today: the
    four organs of every computer — and why knowing them turns your
    tuning instincts into engineering. The specimen is your own
    project folder.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('why did my game stutter on one computer and not another?', 3, 'concept')}

${renderDecompose('9.7', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The four organs — and what your game asks of each:</strong></p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Organ</th><th>Job</th><th>Your game's demand</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>CPU</strong></td><td>Executes instructions — your C#</td><td>Every Update: input, Distance checks, cooldowns, and the scene search that runs on an E press</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>GPU</strong></td><td>Draws pixels — massively parallel</td><td>The tilemaps, the 3,429 sub-sprites in your art folder, your camera view, 60 times a second</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>RAM</strong></td><td>Working memory — fast, forgets at power-off</td><td>The loaded scene, every field, and every texture decoded out of its PNG</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Storage (SSD/HDD)</strong></td><td>Persistent — slow, remembers</td><td>The .unity files, the artwork, your whole project folder (<a href="9.4.html">9.4</a>)</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p><strong>What your project actually costs.</strong> Open the
    folder you have been building in and these are numbers you can
    read yourself, not estimates:</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Measured on the course project</th><th>Size</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Assets/</code> — your source: scripts, art, scenes, prefabs</td>
            <td>36,852 KiB over 4,967 files</td>
            <td>This part is yours, and it is the part a classmate actually needs.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Library/</code> — Unity's import cache</td>
            <td>641,512 KiB over 10,418 files</td>
            <td>Seventeen times the source, and none of it is your work. Unity rebuilds it on the next machine, which is why it is not what you share.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Your nine scripts in <code>Assets/Scripts/</code></td>
            <td>277 lines across 9 files</td>
            <td>Counted, this is the smallest thing in the project — a few pages of text beside the 23,336 KiB of artwork in <code>Assets/Artwork/</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>One sprite sheet: <code>Assets/Artwork/Platformer/Sliced/MainLev2.0.png</code></td>
            <td>589 KB on disk, 1632 &times; 1536 pixels</td>
            <td>Its import settings say uncompressed, so the card holds 2,506,752 pixels at 4 bytes each — about <strong>9.6 MB</strong>. Seventeen times the file.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p><strong>The first table is measured with <code>du -sk</code></strong>
    — run it on your own project folder and you will get your own
    numbers, because the cache grows every time you open the editor.
    The lesson is not the values; it is that they are finite and
    checkable.</p>

    <p><strong>The frame budget.</strong> 60 frames per second is not a
    speed, it is a deadline. 1000 milliseconds divided by 60 frames is
    <strong>16.67 ms</strong>, and everything the machine does for one
    frame has to fit inside it. Ask for 30 fps and the deadline doubles
    to 33.33 ms, which is why half the frame rate is not half the work —
    it is twice the room. A machine 20% slower does not run 20% slower
    either: it either meets the deadline or misses it, and a missed
    deadline is a frame the player sees drop. Your deltaTime habits
    (<a href="1.5.html">1.5</a>) exist because the frame rate is a
    NEGOTIATION with hardware, not a constant.</p>

    <p>How one frame's 16.67 ms divides between the code, the physics and
    the draw is a MEASUREMENT, not a constant — it moves with the scene,
    the machine, and what the player is doing — and taking that
    measurement is what the Profiler is for. The code half below is the
    order those jobs run in, and why the order is a prediction rather
    than a list.</p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Everything the Concept Lab priced is a number you can read out of a window you already
        have open. These six readings are the diagnosis this lesson exists for: which organ ran
        out, and how you know.
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Window → Analysis → Profiler</strong>, and leave it attached while the game
            plays: one bar per frame, and the height of the bar is the 16.67 ms being spent.
            <strong>Profiler → CPU</strong> is where the bar is split into the bands that filled
            it, so your Update cost, the physics cost and the render cost are three bands on one
            bar.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view →</strong> press Play on a scene that is doing almost nothing, and
            read the frame time. On a simple scene most of a quiet frame is render. Then
            <strong>Scene view →</strong> let the turrets fire at once and watch the logic band
            jump: the spike is scheduled work, not a constant cost.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> drag the enemy prefab in and <strong>Ctrl+D</strong> until
            you have 200 of them, each running its own Update and a distance check to the player.
            <strong>Game view →</strong> read the frame rate fall, then
            <strong>Profiler → Hierarchy</strong> to see which method the milliseconds are being
            charged to.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> delete them, and spawn 200 large sprites instead —
            scaled up in the <strong>Scene view</strong> until they cover the camera.
            <strong>Game view →</strong> the frame drops again, and it drops differently: the cost
            is in draw calls rather than in logic, which is what the two bands on the same
            Profiler bar are there to tell apart.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/_Scenes/Overworld</code>, then
            <strong>Game view →</strong> Play. What RAM holds is the scene you loaded: eight
            tilemaps bounding 15,793 cells, of which the layer that actually stops you is 105 by
            91 — 9,555 cells — and the rest are the cave and door alternates of the same level,
            kept in memory so that opening a door is a swap rather than a rebuild.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> the door's <code>SceneSwitch</code> from
            <a href="5.3.html">5.3</a>, then walk through it with the Profiler still open. The old
            scene UNLOADS — that is the hitch you sometimes see mid-door, and it is RAM being
            handed over — and the singleton survives it because
            <code>DontDestroyOnLoad(gameObject)</code> says this one is not scene-RAM any more.
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        The four stages above are the code half of this lesson: your C# occupies exactly one of
        them, and the API below is what decides which.
    </p>

    <p><strong>The frame pipeline, in order:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>1. Input    → your GetAxis/GetKeyDown (Input System)
2. Update  → YOUR code runs (movement, cooldowns, AI ifs)
3. Physics → collision resolution, rigidbody integration
4. Render  → sprites sorted (<a href="7.2.html">7.2</a> layers!) and drawn</code></pre>
    <p>That order is not trivia — it is a prediction. It is why a sound
    played in Update arrives after that frame's physics, and it is why
    the turret's bullet is the clearest example in your own game.
    <code>Turret_Projectile.prefab</code> ships as a Transform, a
    Sprite Renderer and <code>Projectile.cs</code> and nothing else: no
    collider and no Rigidbody2D, so until <a href="3.4.html">3.4</a>
    gave it a shape it was not in the simulation at all. Once you did,
    the hit arrives through <code>OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)</code>
    — and that is a physics callback, so a bullet born during one frame's
    Update cannot be tested against anything until a LATER frame's
    physics pass. Nothing is instant; everything is scheduled.</p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>API</th><th>What it is</th><th>What you get</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Update()</code></td>
            <td>The callback Unity runs once per rendered frame — the one every script you have written this year lives in.</td>
            <td>Work that keeps pace with the frame, because the frame is what calls it.</td>
            <td>Its cost is paid 60 times a second whether or not anything changed, so every check you add here is a per-frame check forever.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>FixedUpdate()</code></td>
            <td>The callback on the physics clock rather than the render clock — the clock <code>Time.fixedDeltaTime</code> measures.</td>
            <td>A steady timestep, so a force applied here is not larger on a slow frame.</td>
            <td>It does not run on every frame, so anything that has to look smooth — input, a cooldown, an animation — stutters if it lives here.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Time.deltaTime</code></td>
            <td>The seconds the last frame took, from <a href="1.5.html">1.5</a> — a measurement of the deadline being met.</td>
            <td>A number that turns per-frame work into per-second work, which is why your movement does not change speed with the frame rate.</td>
            <td>Nothing, except that it reports the past: it describes the frame that just finished, not the one you are in.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: which stage does this work belong in?</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Name the stage each system you built runs in — movement from 1.4, the turret's cooldown from 3.3, the enemy's chase from 4.3, the jump from 6.1 — and the callback that puts it there.</li>
            <li>The bullet's hit is reported in the physics stage of a LATER frame than the Update that spawned it. Name one thing in your own game that depends on that delay, and one bug that would appear if the delay were a frame longer.</li>
            <li>Your frame rate is a negotiation with the hardware, not a constant. Which of the numbers in this lesson would change on a machine twice as fast — and which would not?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            Unity calls Update before physics and FixedUpdate on the
            physics clock. Why would physics need a FIXED timestep
            while your Update does not — and which of your systems
            (a cooldown counting Time.time vs. an <code>AddForce()</code> jump)
            would break if you moved it into the wrong one?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Name the four organs with your game's demand on each</li>
        <li>Do the frame-budget arithmetic and say what a missed deadline is</li>
        <li>Read the Profiler and identify CPU vs GPU saturation</li>
        <li>Explain the frame pipeline order and a bug it predicts</li>
        <li>Say what your project costs on disk and in memory, and why those two numbers differ</li>
        <li>Explain volatile RAM vs persistent storage with your own coins</li>
        <li>Break this question into parts small enough to look each one up on its own, and say in one line what each part does</li>
        <li>Say which earlier lesson each part of it came from, and where you would reread it</li>
    </ul>
</div>

